/**
 * @mainpage USV Digital Twin - Official Documentation
 *
 * @section intro_sec 1. Digital Twin General Flow
 * The **USV Digital Twin** acts as the computational brain for the Unmanned Surface Vehicle. 
 * The overall system flow starts with the ingestion of official S-57 nautical charts, followed by the generation 
 * of a spatial Navigation Mesh (NavMesh) using Constrained Delaunay Triangulation (CDT). 
 * 
 * At runtime, the system maintains a thread-safe `WorldStateSnapshot`. This snapshot unifies the static map 
 * (via R-Tree spatial indexing), the USV real-time odometry, and the predictive kinematics of dynamic targets 
 * to calculate spatial risk fields and predict collisions.
 *
 * @section arch_sec 2. System Architecture (Separation of Concerns)
 * The project is divided into four main packages to isolate the mathematical engine from the communication middleware:
 * 
 * - **`dt_core`**: The pure C++ central engine (ROS-independent). Handles geometric processing (GDAL/Boost), 
 *   Spatial Indexes, memory-safe Snapshot generation, and collision prediction algorithms.
 * - **`dt_msgs`**: Custom message definitions (IDL) for data traffic on the ROS 2 bus.
 * - **`dt_ros`**: The Adapter Node. Acts as a bridge, subscribing to the USV sensors and injecting translated 
 *   raw data into the `dt_core` asynchronously.
 * - **`dt_viz`**: Diagnostic Graphical User Interface (GUI) developed in Qt5.
 *
 * @section qt_gui_sec 3. Qt GUI & Threading Integration
 * To ensure the Qt GUI rendering does not cause *thread-blocking* issues with the ROS 2 spin loop, the architecture 
 * strictly uses the Immutable Snapshot pattern.
 * 
 * The frontend GUI communicates by requesting `DigitalTwinCore::get_latest_state()`. This returns a frozen pointer 
 * of the environment. The Qt application consumes this data via asynchronous Timers and Signals/Slots, querying the 
 * Spatial Engine (O(LogN)) to draw heatmaps, targets, and alerts without locking the backend data ingestion.
 *
 * @section ros_api_sec 4. ROS 2 API (Topics & Interfaces)
 * The `dt_ros` adapter interfaces with the rest of the USV stack through the following topics:
 *
 * @subsection sub_sec Subscribers (Inputs to the Twin)
 * - **`/sensors/gps/fix`** (`sensor_msgs/NavSatFix`): USV global position (Lat/Lon).
 * - **`/sensors/imu/data`** (`sensor_msgs/Imu`): USV orientation (Euler/Quaternions).
 * - **`/sensors/ais/report`** (`dt_msgs/AisReport`): List of tracked dynamic targets in range.
 * - **`/planned_route`** (`nav_msgs/Path`): The current route (waypoints) planned by the navigation stack.
 *
 * @subsection pub_sec Publishers (Outputs from the Twin)
 * - **`/collision_alert`** (`dt_msgs/CollisionAlert`): Consolidated operational alerts containing Time to Closest Point of Approach (TCPA), distance metrics, and computed risk indexes for each target.
 *
 * @section unity_setup_sec 5. Unity Robotics Hub Setup
 *
 * The mission simulation environment uses Unity together with ROS 2 to exchange
 * data with the navigation and Digital Twin modules. Communication between the
 * Unity application and ROS 2 is performed using the Unity Robotics Hub,
 * specifically the ROS-TCP-Connector and ROS-TCP-Endpoint packages.
 *
 * @subsection unity_requirements_sec 5.1 Requirements
 *
 * Before starting the setup, make sure the following components are available:
 *
 * - Unity Hub;
 * - Unity Editor compatible with the Ocean.DigitalTwin project;
 * - ROS 2 Humble or Jazzy;
 * - `colcon`;
 * - `rosdep`;
 * - Git;
 * - ROS-TCP-Connector;
 * - ROS-TCP-Endpoint.
 *
 * @subsection unity_connector_sec 5.2 ROS-TCP-Connector Installation
 *
 * Open the Ocean.DigitalTwin project using Unity Hub.
 *
 * In the Unity Editor, access:
 *
 * @code
 * Window -> Package Manager
 * @endcode
 *
 * In the Package Manager:
 *
 * 1. Click the **+** button;
 * 2. Select **Add package from git URL...**;
 * 3. Insert:
 *
 * @code
 * https://github.com/Unity-Technologies/ROS-TCP-Connector.git?path=/com.unity.robotics.ros-tcp-connector
 * @endcode
 *
 * 4. Click **Add**;
 * 5. Wait for the package installation to finish.
 *
 * After installation, the **Robotics** menu should become available in Unity.
 *
 * @subsection unity_ros_settings_sec 5.3 ROS Settings in Unity
 *
 * In Unity, access:
 *
 * @code
 * Robotics -> ROS Settings
 * @endcode
 *
 * Configure the connection using:
 *
 * - **Protocol:** ROS 2;
 * - **ROS IP Address:** IP address of the environment where ROS 2 is running;
 * - **ROS Port:** 10000.
 *
 * When ROS 2 is running inside WSL, obtain the WSL IP address using:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * hostname -I
 * @endcode
 *
 * The resulting address must be configured in the Unity ROS Settings.
 *
 * @note The WSL IP address may change after restarting WSL. If Unity stops
 * connecting to ROS 2, verify the IP address again.
 *
 * @subsection unity_endpoint_sec 5.4 ROS-TCP-Endpoint Setup
 *
 * The ROS-TCP-Endpoint must run on the ROS 2 side and acts as the TCP bridge
 * between Unity and the ROS graph.
 *
 * Enter the workspace source directory:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * cd ~/usv_digital_twin_ws/src
 * @endcode
 *
 * Clone the ROS 2 version of the endpoint:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * git clone -b ROS2 https://github.com/Unity-Technologies/ROS-TCP-Endpoint.git
 * @endcode
 *
 * Return to the workspace root:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * cd ~/usv_digital_twin_ws
 * @endcode
 *
 * Install the required dependencies:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * rosdep install --from-paths src --ignore-src -r -y
 * @endcode
 *
 * Build the workspace:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * colcon build --symlink-install
 * @endcode
 *
 * Source the ROS 2 environment and the workspace:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash
 * source install/setup.bash
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection unity_endpoint_run_sec 5.5 Starting ROS-TCP-Endpoint
 *
 * Start the endpoint with:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * ros2 run ros_tcp_endpoint default_server_endpoint \
 *   --ros-args -p ROS_IP:=0.0.0.0
 * @endcode
 *
 * The default TCP port used by the Unity Robotics Hub is:
 *
 * @code
 * 10000
 * @endcode
 *
 * Keep this terminal running while the Unity simulation is active.
 *
 * @subsection unity_startup_sec 5.6 Recommended Startup Sequence
 *
 * The recommended startup order is:
 *
 * 1. Start the ROS 2 environment;
 * 2. Source the Digital Twin workspace;
 * 3. Start ROS-TCP-Endpoint;
 * 4. Open Ocean.DigitalTwin in Unity;
 * 5. Verify the settings under `Robotics -> ROS Settings`;
 * 6. Start the simulation using the Unity **Play** button.
 *
 * The communication flow is:
 *
 * @code
 * Ocean.DigitalTwin (Unity)
 *          |
 *          | ROS-TCP-Connector
 *          v
 *      TCP Port 10000
 *          |
 *          v
 *   ROS-TCP-Endpoint
 *          |
 *          v
 *        ROS 2
 *          |
 *          +--> Navigation
 *          +--> Perception
 *          +--> Digital Twin
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection unity_validation_sec 5.7 Communication Validation
 *
 * After starting the environment, verify the ROS 2 nodes:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * ros2 node list
 * @endcode
 *
 * Verify the available topics:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * ros2 topic list
 * @endcode
 *
 * To inspect data from a specific topic:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * ros2 topic echo /topic_name
 * @endcode
 *
 * The Unity/ROS integration can be considered operational when the expected
 * ROS 2 nodes and topics are available and Unity reports no TCP connection
 * errors.
 *
 * @subsection unity_troubleshooting_sec 5.8 Troubleshooting
 *
 * If Unity cannot connect to ROS 2, verify:
 *
 * - ROS-TCP-Endpoint is running;
 * - the IP address configured in Unity is correct;
 * - port `10000` is being used;
 * - the protocol is configured as ROS 2;
 * - Windows Firewall is not blocking the TCP connection;
 * - the workspace has been sourced correctly.
 *
 * If running through WSL, verify the IP again:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * hostname -I
 * @endcode
 *
 * If the ROS 2 topics are not available, source the environments again:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash
 * source ~/usv_digital_twin_ws/install/setup.bash
 * @endcode
 *
 * Then verify:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * ros2 topic list
 * @endcode
 */
